From 6803445a0b14a31de05cf1068c293a9c4ebb8611 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bench <bench@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 02:17:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(eccvm): narrow rows and zero-tail broadcast for
 grand product and log-derivative inverses

The ECCVM grand product and lookup-inverse computations fetched all 119 columns per row via get_row
and evaluated every one of the 2^15 fixed-size rows, although only ~50 / ~21 columns are read and
everything past the active trace (~6.5k rows) is zero.

- ECCVMFlavor::ProverPolynomials gains get_row_for_grand_product / get_row_for_logderivative_inverse
  (only the columns the set / lookup relations read) and the matching *_zero_row_start (first row from
  which all those columns are zero, found by scanning back from each column's end).
- compute_grand_product evaluates numerator/denominator once on the zero row and broadcasts it to all
  later rows; the remaining rows are split evenly over the threads (the active rows previously all fell
  in thread 0's chunk).
- compute_logderivative_inverse stops at the zero row when no lookup operation exists on it.

Same field values row by row, so z_perm and lookup_inverses are unchanged.
